message.content:
{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee, one of the hospitalists. I understand you’ve been having a tough time with nausea and pain since your procedure earlier today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, right after they fixed my femoral line, I started feeling that familiar migraine and then couldn’t stop vomiting—green, bilious stuff. I haven’t been able to keep anything down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And your abdominal pain—is it in the same areas as before?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Exactly the same—left upper quadrant and around my belly button. It’s constant, and I’d say it’s about a 10. It feels just like my past obstruction episodes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. We’ve reviewed your imaging from today—a KUB shows air-fluid levels and dilated bowel, consistent with a partial small bowel obstruction. No free air, which is good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not worse than last time? But why can’t I keep anything down now when I could before?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your gut motility has been chronically impaired, and with the adhesions from your past surgeries, even small changes can trigger this. We’re going to keep your G-tube on suction to help decompress your bowel."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the pain? It’s unbearable. I’ve been on Dilaudid in the ED, but even that barely touches it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re giving you IV Dilaudid in small doses every few hours, just like your ED protocol. We also consulted pain management, but they agree it’s very difficult to control with your allergy history."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I just wish there was something more definitive. I’ve heard about PCA pumps—could that work?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Pain service suggested rectal Dilaudid as an outpatient option since PCA isn’t feasible without a willing primary doctor. We’re going to try a 3 mg suppository as needed every 3 hours when you go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That sounds better than nothing. But surgery—has anyone talked to Dr. ___? I feel like it’s the only real solution."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We consulted general surgery, including your prior surgeon. They feel given your extensive adhesions, another surgery would be too risky and unlikely to help. The risk of volvulus is low because your bowel is so scarred down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I know it’s risky… but living like this isn’t living. I’ve been feeling so hopeless lately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2184-05-21 20:29:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I hear you. We’ve noted your depressive symptoms and will make sure your psychiatrist is looped in. You’re not alone in this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2184-05-22 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs from this morning are stable—your hemoglobin is a bit low at 9.8, but that’s your baseline. No signs of infection or organ failure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2184-05-22 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the liver enzymes? I’ve had Hep C for years."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2184-05-22 08:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Slightly elevated AST and alkaline phosphatase, but nothing acute. We’ll keep monitoring, but it’s likely chronic changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2184-05-22 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. My G-tube is draining a lot—green, fecal-like fluid. Is that normal?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2184-05-22 08:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, that’s expected. It means we’re decompressing your bowel effectively. You’re draining several hundred cc’s a day, which supports the obstruction picture."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2184-05-23 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve been talking with your outpatient team—your GI, pain specialist, and primary doctor. They agree your pain is extremely hard to manage, but options are limited."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2184-05-23 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And? Did they have any new ideas?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2184-05-23 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not really. They support continuing your current meds and adding the rectal Dilaudid at home. We also discussed long-term care or hospice, but I understand you’re not ready for that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2184-05-23 14:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I’m only 50. I want to keep fighting. I know it’s bad, but I’m not giving up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2184-05-23 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And we respect that. You’re a full code, and we’ll continue supporting you at home with TPN, fluids, and symptom control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2184-05-24 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your nausea has improved a bit. We’ve switched from IV Phenergan to liquid form for when you go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2184-05-24 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. I hate shots. Can I start drinking anything?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2184-05-24 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not yet. Your bowel is still sluggish. We’ll keep you on TPN and IV fluids for now. We’re cycling 2.5 liters of saline daily at 150 cc per hour."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2184-05-25 10:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your G-tube output has decreased slightly—from 800 cc to about 500 cc per day. That’s a small sign of improvement."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2184-05-25 10:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s something. My pain is still terrible, though. The IV Dilaudid helps a little, but it fades fast."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2184-05-25 10:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re giving it every 6 hours in a slow infusion. It’s the safest way to manage it here. At home, the suppository should offer more flexibility."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2184-05-26 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning for discharge in a few days. Your labs are stable, and your vitals are normal. You’re still not tolerating food, but that’s expected."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2184-05-26 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m scared to go home. What if I get worse and can’t reach anyone?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2184-05-26 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll continue your weekly ED visits for Dilaudid. And if you vomit more or your pain spikes, come back right away. Your femoral line is stable, so we can give fluids quickly if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2184-05-27 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve finalized your discharge meds. We’re simplifying a few things—lowering your trazodone and amitriptyline doses to reduce sedation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2184-05-27 13:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been so tired. Maybe that’ll help. What about my Abilify? Last time they lowered it for dizziness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2184-05-27 13:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re on 2 mg now, which is your stable dose. We’re keeping it the same. Your dizziness isn’t an issue this admission."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2184-05-28 09:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your latest labs show your hemoglobin dipped to 9.3, but it’s not falling. Your electrolytes are normal, and kidney function is stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2184-05-28 09:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Anemia again. Is it from bleeding?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2184-05-28 09:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No signs of acute bleeding. It’s likely chronic, from your GI losses and nutritional challenges. We’re continuing your iron and multivitamins."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2184-05-29 11:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re scheduling your discharge for tomorrow. You’ll go home with services to help with your TPN and G-tube care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2184-05-29 11:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m nervous, but I guess I have to. Will my pain ever get better?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2184-05-29 11:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We can’t promise that. But we can keep adjusting your comfort. The rectal Dilaudid may give you more control. And we’ll keep talking with your team about new options."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Today’s the day. Your exam shows you’re alert, pain is stable, and your G-tube is draining green material but no signs of infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my heart? I’ve been feeling it race."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your heart rate is in the 80s now—much better than the 120s you usually run. Your EKG was normal, no ischemia. Your mitral valve issue remains stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. I don’t need another thing to worry about."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re sending you home with TPN set to cycle over 12 hours each day—2200 ml with all your nutrients. And 2.5 liters of saline daily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Same as before. I can manage that. And the new suppository—I’ll use it only when the pain is unbearable?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. One 3 mg suppository every 3 hours as needed. And liquid Phenergan for nausea. Call your doctor if you’re using them more than usual."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. And I’ll keep seeing my psychiatrist. I’ve been having dark thoughts, but no plans."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thank you for sharing that. Please keep talking to her. You’re not alone, and we care about your mental health too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I appreciate that. It means a lot."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll have weekly labs—CBC, chem-10, albumin. Fax them to your GI doctor. And follow up with Dr. ___ in two weeks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. I’ll make sure it’s done."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll also keep your femoral line monitored. No redness or issues now, but let us know if it becomes tender."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will do. Thank you, Dr. Lee. I know I’m a tough case, but you’ve been kind."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2184-05-30 08:14:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re strong, and we’re here to support you. Take care, and don’t hesitate to reach out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2184-06-01 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a quick call to check in. How are you holding up at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2184-06-01 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Still nauseous, but the suppository helps the pain a bit. G-tube draining about 400 cc. I used Phenergan twice yesterday."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2184-06-01 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s within expected range. Keep up with fluids and TPN. If output increases or you feel worse, come in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2184-06-01 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks for checking."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re officially closing your admission. You were in for about 12 days, and we’re discharging you from our records now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I’ll keep following the plan."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve been through a lot. Take it one day at a time. We’re here if you need us."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Rest well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 68,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll try."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 69,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 70,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "You too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 71,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Bye for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 72,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Goodbye."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 73,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Stay safe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 74,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 75,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Talk soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 76,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 77,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:14:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care of yourself."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 78,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:15:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll do my best."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 79,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s all we can ask."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 80,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thanks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 81,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 82,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:19:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Goodbye."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 83,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 84,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Bye."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 85,
      "time": "2184-06-02 09:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Rest well."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2184-05-21 19:53:00",
    "admission_end": "2184-06-02 15:00:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a long history of sclerosing mesenteritis, 23 prior abdominal surgeries, and recurrent small bowel obstructions was admitted for worsening nausea, vomiting, and severe abdominal pain consistent with a partial bowel obstruction. She had recently undergone femoral line replacement and developed symptoms en route home. Imaging confirmed bowel dilatation and air-fluid levels, and she was managed conservatively with G-tube suction, TPN, IV fluids, and IV pain control. Surgical evaluation concluded she was not a candidate for further intervention due to extensive adhesions. Pain remained severe despite optimized medical management, and a discharge plan was made with rectal Dilaudid for breakthrough pain. She declined long-term care or hospice, opting to return home with support services. Her depression and suicidal ideation were acknowledged, and outpatient follow-up was arranged. She was discharged in stable condition with ongoing TPN, fluid support, and close monitoring.",
    "problems": [
      "Intestinal or peritoneal adhesions with obstruction (postoperative) (postinfection)",
      "Sclerosing mesenteritis",
      "Chronic hepatitis C without mention of hepatic coma",
      "Gastrostomy status",
      "Migraine with aura, without mention of intractable migraine without mention of status migrainosus",
      "Abdominal pain, right upper quadrant",
      "Abdominal pain, left lower quadrant",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Dyspepsia and other specified disorders of function of stomach",
      "Persistent vomiting"
    ]
  }
}
